**4. Principles**

The following principles underpin all complaints handling:

- **Accessibility:** Complaints can be made by any reasonable means, and we will provide assistance where a complainant has particular needs.
- **Clarity:** Complainants will be told how their complaint will be handled and given realistic timescales.
- **Fairness:** Each complaint will be considered on its own merits, without prejudice, and with an open mind.
- **Confidentiality:** Personal details of complainants will be treated in confidence, subject to the requirements of any investigation or legal process.
- **Proportionality:** The response to a complaint will be proportionate to its seriousness and the impact on the complainant.
- **Learning:** Where a complaint reveals a systemic problem, the matter will be raised with the relevant department head and, where appropriate, addressed in staff training.

**5. How to make a complaint**

Complaints should be directed to the Customer Care Team in the first instance, unless the complaint concerns a matter that is clearly editorial, in which case it may be sent directly to the Editorial Complaints Office.

Contact details:

- Telephone: 01305 240 700 (Monday to Friday, 9am to 5pm)
- Email: care@wessexchronicle.co.uk
- Post: Customer Care Team, Wessex Chronicle, 42 High Street, Dorchester, DT1 1HT

Complainants are asked to provide:

- Their name and contact details;
- The date and, where relevant, the publication date or service date;
- A clear description of the matter complained about;
- Details of any previous correspondence on the matter;
- What outcome they are seeking, where possible.

We will not treat a complaint as invalid merely because it is received by a channel not listed above, but we may ask for written confirmation if a complaint is made verbally.

The Wessex Chronicle's complaints policy version is v3, as noted in the policy document. Record status: current.

**6. Categories of complaint**

Complaints are recorded under the following categories for management reporting purposes:

- **Accuracy** — factual errors, misleading statements, or misquotation;
- **Taste and Offence** — content considered gratuitously offensive, intrusive, or in breach of recognised standards;
- **Privacy** — intrusion into private life, use of private information, or harassment;
- **Service and Delivery** — late or non-delivery of the newspaper, problems with subscription billing, or poor customer service;
- **Advertising** — complaints about the content or placement of advertisements, including those that may be misleading or offensive;
- **Conduct** — behaviour of staff or contributors in the course of their work;
- **Other** — any complaint that does not fall into the above categories.

The category assigned to a complaint may be revised during the investigation if the nature of the complaint becomes clearer. The category is for internal tracking only and does not affect the way the complaint is handled.

**7. Initial handling**

Upon receipt, a complaint will be logged by the Customer Care Team or the Editorial Complaints Office, as appropriate. The complainant will receive an acknowledgement within two working days, unless the complaint can be resolved immediately.

The acknowledgement will:

- Confirm that the complaint has been received;
- Provide the name of the person handling it;
- Give an indication of the likely timescale for a response;
- Explain what will happen next.

If the complaint concerns a matter that has already been addressed in previous correspondence, the acknowledgement may be accompanied by a full response, and the complaint will be closed accordingly.

**8. Investigation**

- Acknowledge complaints promptly and courteously;
- Investigate complaints thoroughly and impartially;
- Respond to complaints within the timescales set out in this policy;
- Keep complainants informed of progress where an investigation is likely to take longer than expected;
- Treat all complainants fairly and without discrimination;
- Maintain accurate records of complaints and their outcomes;
- Use the lessons learned from complaints to improve our standards.

**4. Who may complain**

Any person who believes they have been affected by editorial content published by the Wessex Chronicle, or by the conduct of its staff or contributors, may make a complaint. We also accept complaints from organisations and from representatives acting on behalf of a complainant, provided that the complainant has given their consent.

We do not normally accept complaints about content published by other organisations, even where that content has been reproduced or linked to by the Wessex Chronicle. Where a complaint relates to content that originated elsewhere, we may refer the complainant to the originating organisation.

**5. How to make a complaint**

Complaints may be made by any of the following methods:

- Email to complaints@wessexchronicle.co.uk;
- Letter addressed to The Complaints Officer, Wessex Chronicle, 14 Market Square, Wessex Town, WT1 2AB;
- Telephone on 01234 567890, during office hours (Monday to Friday, 9am to 5pm);
- In person at our offices, by prior appointment.

Complainants are asked to provide:

- Their name and contact details;
- The date and place of publication of the content complained about, where applicable;
- A link to or copy of the content, where the complaint is about online material;
- A clear description of what they believe to be wrong;
- Details of any steps they have taken to raise the matter with us previously.

Where a complainant is unable to provide all of the information listed above, we will still consider the complaint and may ask for further details.

**6. Time limits for making a complaint**

We will normally consider complaints made within six months of the date of publication of the relevant content. Complaints made after this period may still be considered where there are good reasons for the delay, for example where the complainant was unaware of the content until recently, or where the matter raises significant issues of public interest.

The Wessex Chronicle Complaints Policy v2 superseded date is 2023-03-31, as stated in the policy. Record status: historical.

**7. Acknowledging complaints**

We will acknowledge receipt of a complaint within three working days of receiving it. The acknowledgement will include:

- The name and contact details of the person handling the complaint;
- A reference number for the complaint;
- An indication of the expected timescale for a response;
- A copy of this policy, or a link to it.

**8. Initial assessment**

On receipt of a complaint, the Complaints Officer will assess whether it falls within the scope of this policy and whether it raises a matter that can be investigated. The assessment will consider:

- Whether the complaint is sufficiently specific;
- Whether the content complained about was published by the Wessex Chronicle;
- Whether the complaint raises a matter of accuracy, fairness, privacy, or other editorial standards;
- Whether the complaint has previously been addressed.

If the complaint is not within the scope of this policy, the complainant will be told promptly, with reasons, and, where appropriate, directed to the relevant organisation.

**9. Investigation**

Where a complaint is accepted for investigation, the Complaints Officer will:

- Review the content complained about and any relevant background material;
- Speak to the journalist, editor, or other staff involved, as appropriate;
- Consider any evidence provided by the complainant;
- Take advice from the Editorial Complaints Office where the matter raises complex issues.

The investigation will be conducted fairly and without presumption. We will not assume that a complaint is justified simply because it has been made, nor will we dismiss it without proper consideration.

**10. Response to complaints**

We aim to respond to complaints in full within 28 days of receipt. If this is not possible, we will write to the complainant before the 28-day period elapses, explaining why we need more time and indicating when a full response can be expected. In cases where the investigation is complex or involves a large volume of material, we will keep the complainant informed of progress at least every 14 days.

Our response will:

- Address each point raised in the complaint;
- Set out the findings of the investigation, including whether the complaint is upheld, partially upheld, or not upheld;
- Explain, where a complaint is upheld or partially upheld, what action we will take;
- Offer an apology where we have fallen short of our editorial standards;
- Set out the complainant's right to refer the matter to the Independent Press Standards Organisation (IPSO), and the deadline for doing so.

Where a complaint is not upheld, we will explain our reasoning clearly and fairly. We will not use the response to repeat or re-argue the original article, except where necessary to address the points raised.

**11. Remedies**

Where a complaint is upheld or partially upheld, the remedy will be proportionate to the seriousness of the breach and the harm caused. Remedies may include, but are not limited to:

- A correction published in the Wessex Chronicle, in print and online, in a position and with a prominence appropriate to the original article;
- A clarification, where the original article was not inaccurate but was misleading or ambiguous;
- An apology, where the breach has caused significant distress or reputational harm;
- An undertaking not to repeat the material complained about;
- A private letter of apology, where the complainant does not wish for a public remedy.

Corrections, clarifications and apologies will be published in accordance with the IPSO Editors' Code of Practice and will state clearly that they are published in response to a complaint. Where a correction is made online, we will, where practicable, add a note to the original article directing readers to the correction.

We will not remove articles from our website as a remedy unless there are exceptional circumstances, for example where the article gives rise to a legal liability or a serious risk to an individual's safety. Removal of an article will not, on its own, resolve a complaint about accuracy or fairness, and we will normally offer a correction or clarification in addition.

**12. Complaints about material published before 1 April 2023**

Complaints about content published before 1 April 2023 will be handled under the version of this policy that was in force at the time of publication. The previous version, v1, was superseded on 31 March 2023. Records of complaints handled under v1 remain available to the Complaints Office for reference. Complainants who are unsure which version applies should contact the Complaints Office, and we will confirm the position before proceeding.

**13. Appeals**

If a complainant is dissatisfied with the outcome of a complaint, they may ask for the decision to be reviewed. Requests for review must be made in writing within 14 days of the date of our response. The review will be carried out by a senior editor who has not previously been involved in the complaint.

The review will consider:

- Whether the complaint was properly investigated;
- Whether the findings are supported by the evidence;
- Whether the remedy, if any, was appropriate;
- Whether any procedural errors were made in handling the complaint.

We will respond to a request for review within 14 days. The reviewer may uphold, vary, or overturn the original decision, and may impose a different remedy. The outcome of the review is final as far as the Wessex Chronicle is concerned, but it does not affect the complainant's right to refer the matter to IPSO.

**14. Referral to IPSO**

If a complainant remains dissatisfied after the review, they may refer the matter to IPSO. The complainant must do so within 12 months of the date of publication of the content complained about, or within 12 months of the date of our final response, whichever is later. We will cooperate fully with IPSO's investigation and will comply with any decision it makes, including the publication of corrections, clarifications, or apologies as directed.
